<header><p class="st-kicker">county · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Earldom of Ulster</h1><p class="st-meta">1205 CE – 1333 CE · capital Carrickfergus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Anglo-Norman lordship of eastern Ulster founded by John de Courcy and held by the de Burghs, whose collapse in 1333 opened the way to Gaelic recovery.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-de-courcy">John de Courcy</a> invaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-ireland">Ulster</a> in 1177 and ruled it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrickfergus">Carrickfergus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/downpatrick">Downpatrick</a>. In 1205 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> expelled him and created the earldom of Ulster for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-de-lacy-earl-of-ulster">Hugh de Lacy</a>. After de Lacy's death the earldom passed in 1263 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-de-burgh-1st-earl-of-ulster">Walter de Burgh</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The earls held coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-antrim">Antrim</a> and Down with castles, towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrickfergus">Carrickfergus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coleraine">Coleraine</a>, and manors worked by English settlers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-og-de-burgh-2nd-earl-of-ulster">Richard de Burgh</a>, the Red Earl, was the most powerful lord in Ireland around 1300, overlord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-neill-dynasty">O'Neills</a> and lord of much of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-connacht">Connacht</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-bruce">Edward Bruce's</a> invasion of 1315 to 1318 devastated the earldom.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1333 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-donn-de-burgh-3rd-earl-of-ulster">William Donn de Burgh</a>, the Brown Earl, was murdered by his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> at a ford near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a>. The earldom passed by marriage to the English royal family, but Gaelic lords such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clandeboye-oneills">O'Neills of Clandeboye</a> reoccupied most of its lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/earldom-of-ulster.jpg" alt="Earldom of Ulster" width="320" height="374"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_House_of_de_Burgh.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1205 CE – 1333 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Carrickfergus</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John de Courcy, Hugh de Lacy, Richard de Burgh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Anglo-Norman French, Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Liberty of Ulster</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1269&amp;focus=earldom-of-ulster" aria-label="Earldom of Ulster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4552 305 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Earldom of Ulster lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4872 561L4861 599M4872 561L4874 563M4872 561L4809 603"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4861 599h0M4874 563h0M4809 603h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4872 561h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4872 561h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Carrickfergus. <a href="/atlas?year=1269&amp;focus=earldom-of-ulster">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
